summary.text:
I need write CODESYS ST function block exact interface and behavior, use compiler. Should decide timers TIME(), perhaps dt elapsed wall time previous invocation. Should have implementation no external dt. Use TON standard with PT T#5S and cooldown TON. Scan semantics initial conditions ghost. TON elapsed zero on first eligible sampled invocation aligns standard TON. But subtle continuous heating timing and protection trip, restart same scan at >=3. TON behavior CODESYS standard. Could implement with TON and standard. Make sure heating timer tracks actual HeaterOutput pre-scan? Tests: first scan demand true, timer IN before output computed? I can compute demand, use previous output? Best structure timer heatTON(IN := HeaterOutput, PT 5s) at beginning; initial HeaterOutput false so at first energized sample timer not invoked IN true until next scan. Test second at dt4999: heat timer first eligible at second scan elapsed zero, then third 1ms no trip -> wrong. Should invoke timer based on current demand before final output, so first scan starts timer ET zero. Then dt4999 second elapsed 4999; third +1=5000 trip. Good.

At protection active, cooldown TON IN OverheatProtection, first trip scan should elapsed zero, subsequent dt2999 then +1 clears. Should call cooldown timer after setting protection on trip so starts same scan. Or pre-call with previous OverheatProtection would first eligible next scan => off by one. So call after trip evaluation.

Potential standard TON semantics: FB call updates Q and ET; when IN false resets. On trip scan set latch true and call. At cooldown completed, clear latch and reset timer perhaps call again IN false? Not necessary next scan if protection false resets. Can restart same scan. Heat timer while protection active should be IN false reset. On clearing scan, heat timer IN demand true gets first elapsed zero, heater on. Great.

Should demand update at equality retain. Sensor fault demand? Assumption fault turns heat off resets continuous time; does not specify demand updating during fault. likely demand comparisons regardless? Tests T2 fault -0.1 then 100 then fault 100.1 then equality: initial demand true from 0; at -0.1 if update demand true; 100 >30 clears; 100.1 clears; equality retains false => expected false. If don't update on fault, demand remains true and equality would true, failing. So update demand regardless fault. At T1 after trip and cooldown demand remains true. At final 31 clears.

Heat timer on sensor fault reset. During overheat latch reset. Should avoid heat timer ET >=5 causing persistentre-trip after cooldown. Sequence:
display, demand update, sensorFault.
If OverheatProtection preexisting then perhaps heatTON IN false. Determine trip based on heat timer only when permitted and demand. I can call heatTON(IN := heatDemand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection, PT5). If Q then latch true. Then cooldownTON(IN := OverheatProtection,...). If OverheatProtection and cooldownTON.Q then clear. Then output demand etc.
At trip scan: heatTON called true -> Q true at 5sec, set protection. cooldown called true starts. output false.
Next scans: heatTON IN false reset. cooldown continues.
At cooldown done scan: heatTON called IN false (pre protection), resets. cooldown Q true, clear protection. output true. Next scan heat timer starts, meaning first energized sample at cooldown clearing scan wasn't counted. Requirement says "heat can restart in same scan", and heat continuous elapsed first energized sample zero. Should heat timer be invoked true on clearing scan to start at zero; next scan dt should count from clearing. If not, next scan starts at zero and threshold delayed one sample. Tests don't cover post restart trip but spec implies. Should have robust structure handle cooldown first then heating? On trip scan need cooldown start same scan. Can order:
- Update demand/fault
- Handle existing protection cooldown: cooldownTON(IN := OverheatProtection), if Q clear. But trip scan cooldown won't start until next scan -> tests fail.
Could start cooldown manually at trip with additional call? Calling TON twice per scan bad.
Could structure protection state and timers with cooling called after possible trip, and heating called after cooling clearing, but heating Q needed trip before cooling. Could use previous heat timer Q? Or call heat timer before, and after clear call again IN true to reset/start? Double calls may be problematic and elapsed semantics. Better implement own timers using TIME() and 64-bit? CODESYS TIME is 32-bit milliseconds wrap. Could use LTIME? portability. Standard TON and logic with a state flag. Could call heat timer with `heatTimingEnable` calculated based on pre protection and cooldown Q impossible before cooling call unless use previous ET/Q. Could evaluate cooldown timer at beginning on every scan, including protection from previous. On trip scan, separately first call after setting? Alternative detect trip using heat timer then cooling call. At beginning if OverheatProtection call cooldownTON. On new trip later call cooldownTON? double only trip scan first was not called if pre false. So one call per scan: if pre protection then at beginning, else after tripwith IN current protection. On pre protection completion, clear before heat timer, enabling heat and starts same scan. On regular no protection, cooling TON IN false at beginning, heat timer later. This is clean but branches complex.

Pseudo:
Current... demand...
sensorFault...
IF OverheatProtection THEN
  cooldownTON(IN:=TRUE, PT:=3S);
  IF cooldownTON.Q THEN OverheatProtection:=FALSE; END_IF;
ELSE
  cooldownTON(IN:=FALSE, PT:=3S);
END_IF;

heatTON(IN := heatDemand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection, PT5);
IF heatTON.Q THEN
  OverheatProtection:=TRUE;
END_IF;

IF OverheatProtection AND NOT cooldownTON.Q? On trip scan cooldown currently called false at beginning because pre false. Now start cooldown after trip: `IF OverheatProtection AND NOT protectPrev THEN cooldownTON(IN:=TRUE,...); END_IF;` One extra only on trip. On pre protection clearing scan, OverheatProtection false, heat timer starts. Output.
On trip scan heat timer Q true and protection true. output false. Cooldown ET zero.
On pre protection active not done, heat timer false. good.
On sensor fault during protection, cooling continues. Demand update. heat reset.
On pre protection cooldown done and sensor invalid, clear protection anyway after >=3 as spec may clear, output false. fine.
At exact trip after prior heating? heat timer Q. Set heatDemand retained. Output computed `heatDemand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection AND NOT heatTON.Q` though protection covers Q. reset heat elapsed? heat timer remains Q until next scan but protection reset after 3 and heat call false during protection resets. Fine.

Could simplify using TON and protectPrev variable. Should have declaration includes VAR_INPUT, VAR_OUTPUT, VAR heatDemand BOOL, sensorFault BOOL, heatTimer TON, cooldownTimer TON, wasProtected BOOL perhaps. No need initial values explicit default false. `wasProtected := OverheatProtection;` at start after displays. Then cooldown logic based on wasProtected:
IF wasProtected THEN
 cooldownTimer(IN:=TRUE,PT:=T#3S);
 IF cooldownTimer.Q THEN OverheatProtection:=FALSE; END_IF;
ELSE
 cooldownTimer(IN:=FALSE,PT:=T#3S);
END_IF;
heatTimer(IN := heatDemand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection, PT:=T#5S);
IF heatTimer.Q THEN OverheatProtection := TRUE; END_IF;
IF OverheatProtection AND NOT wasProtected THENcooldownTimer(IN:=TRUE,...); END_IF;
HeaterOutput := heatDemand AND NOT sensorFault AND NOT OverheatProtection;
HeaterStatus := HeaterOutput.

Question timer call condition and first eligible sampled invocation: TON calls IN false on initial equality etc. Fine.

Potential CODESYS TON type available standard. Compile. Perhaps function block declaration no END_FUNCTION_BLOCK per instructions. implementation ST body alone. supporting_objects []. I'll call.

